          %h1#basic_localizers Basic localizers
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#asphericity_and_local_equivalences"} Asphericity and local equivalences
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#selfduality"} Self-duality
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#cisinskis_theorem"} Cisinski’s theorem
                %ul
                  %li
                    %a{:href => "#theorem_cisinski"} Theorem (Cisinski)
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            By a
            %em basic localizer
            one means a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizer"} localizer
            on the category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functors"} functors
            to be called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
          %p
            These conditions ensure in particular that a basic localizer always contains the weak equivalences of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomason+model+structure"} Thomason model structure
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Maltsiniotis11"} Maltsiniotis 11, 1.2.1
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            at which is equivalent to the standard
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences+of+categories"} equivalences of categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+model+structure"} canonical model structure
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
          %p
            Hence basic localizers are a tool for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            modeled on
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            was motivated from the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/test+categories"} test categories
            (see remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CitationOnTestCategories"}
            below).
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            The definition is due to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
          #BasicLocalizer.num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong basic localizer
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
              in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
              such that
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                  contains all
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identities"} identities
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-out-of-3+property"} 2-out-of-3 property
                  and, for any functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\colon A\to B
                  such that there exists a functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi B
                        %mo →
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r\colon B\to A
                  with
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi r
                        %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r i=1_A
                  and
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi r
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i r
                  in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi W
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
                  in
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                  (in the literature this is sometimes called being
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %em weakly saturated
              %li
                %p
                  If
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                  has a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo →
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to 1
                  is in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi W
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              %li
                %p
                  Given a commutative triangle in
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi Cat
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi A
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mover
                                  %mo →
                                  %mi u
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mi B
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mo
                                  %mi v
                                %mo ↘
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %msub
                                  %mo ↙
                                  %mi w
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{ A &amp; &amp; \overset{u}{\to} &amp; &amp; B\\ &amp; _v \searrow &amp; &amp; \swarrow_w \\ &amp; &amp; C }
                %p
                  if each induced functor
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi v
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi c
                        %mo →
                        %mi w
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi c
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v/c \to w/c
                  between
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comma+categories"} comma categories
                  is in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi W
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi u
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
                  is also in
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi W
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The term in French is
              = succeed "," do
                %strong localisateur fondamental
              = succeed "." do
                %strong fundamental localizer
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              One can show that basic localizers are stable under
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/retracts"} retracts
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#Cisinski06"} Cisinski 06
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#Cisinski06"} Cisinski 06
          #CitationOnTestCategories.num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              In
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"} Pursuing Stacks
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
              wrote about def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BasicLocalizer"}
              the following:
            %blockquote
              %p
                These conditions are enough, I quickly checked this night, in order to validify all results developed so far on
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/test+categories"} test categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+test+categories"} weak test categories
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+test+categories"} strict test categories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Cat
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Cat)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i(a)
                has a final object. All this I believe is justification enough for the definition above.
          #weakBasicLocalizer.num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              Grothendieck’s comments above refer in fact to a weaker definition, in which in Definition
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BasicLocalizer"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon A\to B
              such that, for any object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi b
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A/b\to 1
              is in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              = succeed "." do
                %strong weak basic localizer
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The class of
                %em all
                functors between small categories is, of course, the maximal basic localizer.
            %li
              %p
                The class of functors inducing an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+components"} connected components
                is a basic localizer.
            %li
              %p
                The class of functors whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
                is a basic localizer. (These are the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
                in the
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomason+model+structure"} Thomason model structure
            %li
              %p
                For any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivator"} derivator
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
                = succeed "-equivalences" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi D
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %li
              %p
                The class of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalences+of+categories"} equivalences of categories
                is
                %em not
                a basic localizer (it fails the second condition). (These are the weak equivalences of the
                = succeed ".)" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+model+structure"} canonical model structure
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#asphericity_and_local_equivalences Asphericity and local equivalences
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            is a basic localizer, we define the following related classes. We sometimes refer to functors in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %em weak equivalences
          %ul
            %li
              A category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is
              %strong
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                \-)aspherical
              if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to 1
              is in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %li
              A functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u\colon A\to B
              is
              %strong
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                \-)aspherical
              if for all
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in B
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u/b
              is aspherical.
            %li
              When the hypotheses of the third axiom are satisfied, we say that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %strong
                  local weak equivalence over
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi C
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              If
              = succeed "-equivalences," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W = \pi_0
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+functor"} initial
          .num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo =
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W =
              nerve equivalences, then a category is aspherical iff its nerve is contractible, and a functor is aspherical iff it is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+initial+functor"} homotopy initial
          %p We observe the following.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                A category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                is aspherical iff the functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\to 1
                is aspherical, since the only comma category involved in the latter assertion is
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                itself.
            %li
              %p
                An aspherical functor is a weak equivalence. For if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi A
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u\colon A\to B
                is aspherical, then consider the triangle
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %mi A
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mi u
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mi B
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %msub
                                %mo
                                %mi u
                              %mo ↘
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo ↙
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \array{ A &amp; &amp; \overset{u}{\to} &amp; &amp; B\\ &amp; _u \searrow &amp; &amp; \swarrow \\ &amp; &amp; B }
              %p
                The third axiom tells us to consider, for a given
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi b
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi B
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in B
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi u
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi b
                        %mo →
                        %mi B
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi b
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u/b \to B/b
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u/b
                is aspherical by assumption, while
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B/b
                is aspherical by the second axiom since it has a terminal object. Thus, by 2-out-of-3, the functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi b
                      %mo →
                      %mi B
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u/b \to B/b
                is in
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
                is in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
                has a right adjoint, then it is aspherical. For in this case, each category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi u
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u/b
                has a terminal object, and thus is aspherical.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
                denotes the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval+category"} interval category
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                the projection
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi I
                      %mo →
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times I\to A
                has a right adjoint, hence is aspherical and thus a weak equivalence. By 2-out-of-3, the two injections
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \rightrightarrows A\times I
                are also weak equivalences, so
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ×
                      %mi I
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A\times I
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cylinder+object"} cylinder object
                for
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi W
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo →
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\to g
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                is in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
                if and only if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                is. Moreover, if
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                is a “homotopy equivalence” in the sense that it has an “inverse”
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mi g
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f g
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g f
                are connected to identities by arbitrary natural zigzags, then
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                is a weak equivalence.
            %li
              %p In particular, any left or right adjoint is a weak equivalence.
          %h3#selfduality Self-duality
          %p It is a non-obvious fact that the notion of basic localizer is self-dual.
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              A functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi u
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u : A \to B
              is in a basic localizer
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
              if and only if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi u
                      %mi op
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi B
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u^{op} : A^{op} \to B^{op}
              is in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              See Proposition 1.1.22 in (
              %a{:href => "#Maltsiniotis05"}> Maltsiniotis 05
              ).
          %h3#cisinskis_theorem Cisinski’s theorem
          %p
            Since the definition consists merely of closure conditions, the intersection of any family of basic localizers is again a basic localizer. It follows that there is a unique
            %em smallest
            basic localizer. The following was conjectured by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            and proven by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"} Denis-Charles Cisinski
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_cisinski Theorem (Cisinski)
            %p
              The class of functors whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nerve"} nerve
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalence"} weak homotopy equivalence
              is the smallest basic localizer.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              See Théorème 2.2.11 in (
              %a{:href => "#Cisinski04"}> Cisinski 04
              ). A completely different proof is given in Corollaire 4.2.19 in (
              %a{:href => "#Cisinski06"}> Cisinski 06
              )
          %p
            Note that this is a larger class than the class of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalences"}> homotopy equivalences
            ” considered above. For instance, the category generated by the graph
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                  %mo ←
                  %mo •
                  %mo →
                  %mo •
                  %mo ←
                  %mo •
                  %mo →
                  %mo •
                  %mo ←
                  %mo •
                  %mo →
                  %mi ⋯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet \leftarrow \bullet \rightarrow \bullet \leftarrow \bullet \rightarrow \bullet \leftarrow \bullet \rightarrow \cdots
          %p has a contractible nerve, but its identity functor is not connected to a constant one by any natural zigzag.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              One can in fact prove that the class of functors whose nerve is a weak homotopy equivalence is the smallest weak basic localizer, as defined in Remark
              = succeed ";" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#weakBasicLocalizer"}
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#Cisinski06"} Cisinski 06
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#Cisinski09"} Cisinski 09
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#Cisinski06"} Cisinski 06
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+model+structure"}> canonical model structure
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomason+model+structure"} Thomason model structure
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cisinski+model+structure"} Cisinski model structure
          %h2#references References
          %ul#Cisinski06
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/joyalscatlab/published/Les+pr%C3%A9faisceaux+comme+type+d%27homotopie"} Les préfaisceaux comme type d'homotopie
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/AST_2006__308__R1_0/"} numdam
          %ul#Cisinski04
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Le localisateur fondamental minimal
              %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/CTGDC_2004__45_2_109_0/"} numdam
          %ul#Cisinski09
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Locally constant functors
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0803.4342"} arXiv
          %ul#Maltsiniotis05
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Maltsiniotis"}> Georges Maltsiniotis
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em La théorie de l’homotopie de Grothendieck
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/AST_2005__301__R1_0/"} numdam
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cisinski+model+structure"} Cisinski model structure
          %ul#Maltsiniotis11
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Maltsiniotis"}> Georges Maltsiniotis
              ,
              %em Homotopical exact squares and derivators
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1101.4144"}> arXiv:1101.4144
              )
